WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.002
Hello, everyone! I'm Mr. Daniel. Welcome to Online Intermediate.

2
00:00:05.302 --> 00:00:08.584
Hi! I'm Nita. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:08.884 --> 00:00:16.052
Unit twenty-five: Getting Around. In this unit, you will talk about transport and make guesses about situations.

4
00:00:17.050 --> 00:00:18.608
ride-hailing.

5
00:00:19.308 --> 00:00:22.794
ride-hailing means booking a car or tuk-tuk ride with a phone app.

6
00:00:23.094 --> 00:00:25.662
Ride-hailing apps are very popular in Phnom Penh.

7
00:00:26.362 --> 00:00:29.328
Your turn. Say: ride-hailing.

8
00:00:32.028 --> 00:00:33.169
ride-hailing.

9
00:00:34.183 --> 00:00:35.080
toll road.

10
00:00:35.780 --> 00:00:37.988
toll road means a road that you pay to use.

11
00:00:38.288 --> 00:00:40.903
The new toll road to Sihanoukville saves hours.

12
00:00:41.603 --> 00:00:43.756
Your turn. Say: toll road.

13
00:00:46.456 --> 00:00:47.446
toll road.

14
00:00:48.450 --> 00:00:49.486
speed camera.

15
00:00:50.186 --> 00:00:54.194
speed camera means a camera that photographs vehicles going too fast.

16
00:00:54.494 --> 00:00:56.401
There's a speed camera on this bridge.

17
00:00:57.101 --> 00:00:59.765
Your turn. Say: speed camera.

18
00:01:02.465 --> 00:01:03.617
speed camera.

19
00:01:04.617 --> 00:01:05.699
parking fine.

20
00:01:06.399 --> 00:01:10.059
parking fine means money you must pay for parking in the wrong place.

21
00:01:10.359 --> 00:01:12.521
I got a parking fine outside the bank.

22
00:01:13.221 --> 00:01:15.757
Your turn. Say: parking fine.

23
00:01:18.457 --> 00:01:19.598
parking fine.

24
00:01:20.583 --> 00:01:21.666
road sign.

25
00:01:22.366 --> 00:01:26.142
road sign means a sign that gives information to drivers.

26
00:01:26.442 --> 00:01:28.371
Always follow the road signs.

27
00:01:29.071 --> 00:01:31.538
Your turn. Say: road sign.

28
00:01:34.238 --> 00:01:35.390
road sign.

29
00:01:36.383 --> 00:01:37.245
detour.

30
00:01:37.945 --> 00:01:42.162
detour means a different, longer route because the normal road is closed.

31
00:01:42.462 --> 00:01:44.764
There's a detour because of the road works.

32
00:01:45.464 --> 00:01:47.454
Your turn. Say: detour.

33
00:01:50.154 --> 00:01:51.074
detour.

34
00:01:52.083 --> 00:01:53.131
breakdown.

35
00:01:53.831 --> 00:01:56.469
breakdown means when a vehicle stops working.

36
00:01:56.769 --> 00:01:58.873
We had a breakdown on the way to Kampot.

37
00:01:59.573 --> 00:02:01.749
Your turn. Say: breakdown.

38
00:02:04.449 --> 00:02:05.439
breakdown.

39
00:02:06.450 --> 00:02:07.405
flat tyre.

40
00:02:08.105 --> 00:02:10.580
flat tyre means a tyre with no air in it.

41
00:02:10.880 --> 00:02:12.973
I had to change a flat tyre in the rain.

42
00:02:13.673 --> 00:02:15.895
Your turn. Say: flat tyre.

43
00:02:18.595 --> 00:02:19.759
flat tyre.

44
00:02:20.750 --> 00:02:22.784
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:02:23.084 --> 00:02:26.317
The bus is very late. There must be a traffic jam.

46
00:02:27.617 --> 00:02:29.721
Or it might have had a breakdown.

47
00:02:31.017 --> 00:02:33.495
It can't be the weather. It's sunny!

48
00:02:34.783 --> 00:02:37.993
Look, there's a sign for a detour. That must be the reason.

49
00:02:39.283 --> 00:02:43.140
Maybe I should use a ride-hailing app instead.

50
00:02:44.450 --> 00:02:47.555
Good idea. It might be faster than waiting here.

51
00:02:48.850 --> 00:02:51.093
Grammar focus: Modals of deduction.

52
00:02:51.393 --> 00:02:53.532
Use must when you are sure something is true.

53
00:02:53.832 --> 00:02:57.515
Use might, may or could when it's possible, and can't when you're sure it's not true.

54
00:02:57.815 --> 00:03:01.489
For example: He must be stuck in traffic. It can't be the weather.

55
00:03:01.789 --> 00:03:06.299
Use mustn't for rules, not for deduction. For deduction, the negative is can't.

56
00:03:07.283 --> 00:03:10.110
Now read and listen. Traffic in a Growing City.

57
00:03:10.410 --> 00:03:13.524
Phnom Penh has more than two million motorbikes and cars.

58
00:03:14.817 --> 00:03:17.281
In the morning rush, a short trip can take an hour.

59
00:03:18.583 --> 00:03:22.057
Many people now use ride-hailing apps to avoid parking problems.

60
00:03:23.350 --> 00:03:26.685
New speed cameras and road signs aim to make the roads safer.

61
00:03:27.983 --> 00:03:32.282
Experts say better public transport must be the long-term solution.

62
00:03:33.583 --> 00:03:35.980
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

63
00:03:36.280 --> 00:03:38.257
A road that you pay to use is a blank.

64
00:03:38.557 --> 00:03:42.071
A, detour. B, toll road. C, road sign.

65
00:03:46.083 --> 00:03:48.036
The answer is B: toll road.

66
00:03:49.550 --> 00:03:52.075
She's not answering. She blank be asleep.

67
00:03:52.375 --> 00:03:55.401
A, must. B, can't. C, mustn't.

68
00:03:59.417 --> 00:04:01.625
The answer is A: must.

69
00:04:03.117 --> 00:04:05.606
That blank be Dara. He's in Japan this week.

70
00:04:05.906 --> 00:04:08.422
A, must. B, might. C, can't.

71
00:04:12.417 --> 00:04:13.952
The answer is C: can't.

72
00:04:15.450 --> 00:04:17.856
When a vehicle stops working, it is a blank.

73
00:04:18.156 --> 00:04:22.076
A, breakdown. B, parking fine. C, flat tyre.

74
00:04:26.083 --> 00:04:27.827
The answer is A: breakdown.

75
00:04:29.317 --> 00:04:32.605
According to the text, Phnom Penh has more than blank vehicles.

76
00:04:32.905 --> 00:04:36.941
A, two hundred thousand. B, two million. C, twenty million.

77
00:04:40.950 --> 00:04:42.833
The answer is B: two million.

78
00:04:44.350 --> 00:04:48.491
Now it's your turn to speak. Your friend is an hour late. What might have happened?

79
00:04:53.791 --> 00:04:59.079
He must be stuck in traffic, or he might have had a flat tyre. It can't be the rain, because it's sunny.

80
00:04:59.379 --> 00:05:00.879
Excellent answer, Nita!

81
00:05:01.883 --> 00:05:05.801
Great work today! You learned eight new words and modals of deduction.

82
00:05:06.101 --> 00:05:09.694
Today's value is patience. Wait calmly and take your time.

83
00:05:09.994 --> 00:05:12.669
See you in Unit twenty-six. Goodbye!

84
00:05:12.969 --> 00:05:15.680
Thank you, Mr. Daniel! Bye, everyone!

